PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core rule preserved: ESA policies (disconnect-from-work + electronic monitoring) + 30-day distribution duty triggered only by >=25 employees on Jan 1 snapshot of the relevant year; status reassessed annually, mid-year drops do not carry forward obligation. 

Round-1 split: D group (colleagues 1,4) claims perpetual duty once triggered in 2022, so D (30 on 1 Jan 2022) remains non-compliant for missing electronic-monitoring policy even at 24 employees in 2023. C group (2,3,5,6,8,9) correctly rejects this, noting annual reset means D has no 2023 duty; C alone exceeds threshold yet violates timing (monthly revisions + quarterly distribution >30 days). B group (7,10) argues voluntary policies still trigger distribution duty, but this is unsupported—below-threshold employers have no statutory obligation. 

Evidence favors C: statute language repeatedly ties both creation and ongoing duties to the Jan 1 count for that calendar year; C is sole employer above threshold that breaches distribution window. Preserve: annual snapshot mechanics, 30-day rule details, and that only C meets threshold while non-compliant.}

